Guess what that's all about?

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.590
My favorite subject .. Swatching.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:30.250
The classroom is just a compilation of
everything that we've have on the website

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:38.030
It's just a great place to just pull it
all together and help you find information

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.980
and really really really
encourage you to knit swatches.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.610
You know, I've never understood why
swatching is a problem for Knitters,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.400
especially machine Knitters.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:51.680
Oh my gosh.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.290
What does it take you 10
minutes to knit a Swatch?

00:00:54.735 --> 00:00:55.175
Really?

00:00:55.265 --> 00:00:56.335
There's no excuse.

00:00:57.035 --> 00:01:00.945
And without it how do you
know how much yarn you need?

00:01:01.945 --> 00:01:07.215
How do you know how this this yarn is
going to knit up and whether your are even

00:01:07.215 --> 00:01:08.345
going to like it?

00:01:08.715 --> 00:01:13.914
How do you know that this thing is going
to turn into something gorgeous and

00:01:13.914 --> 00:01:14.925
wonderful?

00:01:15.065 --> 00:01:18.245
You can take it on
faith or you can swatch.

00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:22.140
Going back to washing.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.710
Let's say you would wash this
thing and it falls apart.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.910
How do you know until you actually try it?

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.790
I'd hate for you to knit a whole sweater
and put all that time and energy in and

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.060
number one: not like it.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:34.890
Number two:.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:35.740
It doesn't fit.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:37.370
Number three: It shrinks.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:41.650
Number four: the yarn biases
or bleeds or something.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.875
No, make a Swatch ahead of time.

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:47.214
Take that 10 minutes and do it.

00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:50.155
So, you know exactly what you're getting.

00:01:52.494 --> 00:01:57.335
In the new classroom again,
it's knititnow.com/Swatch.

00:01:58.044 --> 00:01:58.945
We have tips.

00:01:59.794 --> 00:02:06.054
We've got tips about tension,
machine specific tips.

00:02:07.304 --> 00:02:10.725
Did you know that there are a couple
of different types of swatches?

00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:17.484
One is a tension Swatch, you know, you
get a new cone or a ball of yarn and you

00:02:17.484 --> 00:02:20.005
really just don't know
what tension to Knit it.

00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:23.835
Well, the best way is to
knit a tension Swatch.

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:28.625
Cast on and knit in p attern pattern at
maybe the tightest tension you think that

00:02:28.625 --> 00:02:29.845
that yarn will take.

00:02:30.425 --> 00:02:35.345
Knit for a couple of inches, loosen
up knit for a couple of inches.

00:02:36.215 --> 00:02:42.585
Loosen up knit for a couple inches until
you get to it to a Swatch or tension

00:02:42.585 --> 00:02:44.335
that's really loose and sloppy.

00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:50.575
Drop the thing off the machine and you're
going to know immediately how it feels

00:02:50.575 --> 00:02:54.375
whether you like that at
a specific tension or not.

00:02:55.215 --> 00:03:00.365
And if it's a brand new yarn to you and
maybe is a little iffy go ahead and wash

00:03:00.365 --> 00:03:04.735
it and treat it how you think
you're going to treat your garment.

00:03:05.085 --> 00:03:10.175
So if there's any shrinkage that comes
up, now's the time to learn about it.

00:03:11.355 --> 00:03:14.735
Once you determine how you're
going to knit your Swatch.

00:03:15.265 --> 00:03:18.245
You can work a gauge Swatch.

00:03:18.695 --> 00:03:23.205
Now a gauge swatch for machine
Knitters is just really easy.

00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:23.605
Come on.

00:03:23.635 --> 00:03:26.915
Don't tell me that you're
still counting stitches right?

00:03:27.335 --> 00:03:27.885
Hand hitters.

00:03:27.915 --> 00:03:33.435
I see them laboriously counting stitches
and Counting rows no, no, no, no, no, no

00:03:33.665 --> 00:03:37.595
machine Knitters mark specific area.

00:03:37.595 --> 00:03:43.485
We isolate a specific area
on our Swatch with markers.

00:03:44.485 --> 00:03:51.655
Right and markers on the rows and you know
exactly how many stitches and rows in that

00:03:51.795 --> 00:03:52.845
isolated area.

00:03:52.875 --> 00:03:55.885
You measure it and you get your gauge.

00:03:56.105 --> 00:03:58.795
No counting easy peasy.

00:03:59.125 --> 00:04:01.195
No counting a stitches and rows.

00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:08.385
So once you do that, You measure your
Swatch you dress it first, you know you

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:11.125
wash it block it whatever
you're going to do with it.

00:04:11.505 --> 00:04:13.505
Then you get your gauge.

00:04:13.755 --> 00:04:14.835
And from there.

00:04:15.045 --> 00:04:19.974
You really know that your garment's going
to fit especially if you're using a Knit

00:04:19.974 --> 00:04:24.225
it Now Dynamic pattern, you don't
have to match anybody's gauge.

00:04:24.795 --> 00:04:28.595
Put in your gauge and generate
your own custom pattern.

